/*
 * Core internal modules — the eight slots every hull must fill (armour, power plant, thrusters, FSD, life support, power distributor, sensors, fuel tank).
 *
 * The `core` slice of Frontier's outfitting catalogue: each module's identity
 * AND its engineering/mechanical stats, joined on the Frontier `symbol`.
 *
 * Sources: EDCD FDevIDs, outfitting.csv (identity: symbol, category, name, class,
 *          rating, mount?, guidance?, ship?, entitlement?)
 *          https://github.com/EDCD/FDevIDs
 *          EDCD/coriolis-data, modules/** (stats: mass, power, performance, …)
 *          https://github.com/EDCD/coriolis-data
 *          EDSY eddb.js (taleden, CC BY-NC 4.0): https://github.com/taleden/EDSY
 *          — mass, integrity, powerDraw and bootTime where coriolis-data carries
 *          none, and the corrections where its value is a duplicated or mis-copied
 *          row. The few records no third-party registry lists (the size-8 frame
 *          shift drives and the `*_free` starter fittings) were read from the live
 *          game's own outfitting panels and are pinned in
 *          fixtures/ships/module-stats.json.
 *          The stat values are Elite Dangerous game data, property of Frontier
 *          Developments plc, redistributed under Frontier's media-usage terms:
 *          https://forums.frontier.co.uk/threads/510879/
 *
 * `symbol` is the internal identifier (e.g. `Hpt_PulseLaser_Fixed_Small`); `name`
 * the display name; `class` the module size (0-8) and `rating` its grade letter
 * (A-I) — the "5A" the outfitting screen shows. Identity fields always present;
 * every stat field is optional, present only where the module has it (masses in
 * tonnes, power in MW, jump ranges in LY). `restrictedToShips` lists the hull(s) a
 * non-armour module is limited to; armour's hull restriction is its `ship` field.
 *
 * Defence stats: `kineticResistance`, `thermalResistance`, `explosiveResistance` and
 * `causticResistance` are fractions of damage removed (negative = a weakness);
 * `hullBoost` is an armour variant's bonus as a fraction of the hull's base armour
 * (0.8 means base armour x 1.8), `hullReinforcement` is hull points added,
 * `shieldAddition` shield megajoules added, `moduleProtection` the fraction of module
 * damage absorbed. `alwaysPowered` marks a hardpoint-mounted module that draws its
 * power even with hardpoints retracted.
 * Weapon stats: `damage` is per round (per second on continuous-fire beam and mining
 * lasers, which carry no `rateOfFire`), `damageDistribution` splits it by type,
 * `rateOfFire` is shots per second with the burst pattern and charge time folded in,
 * `distributorDraw` (MW) and `thermalLoad` are per shot, and `maximumRange` /
 * `falloffRange` / `shotSpeed` are metres and metres per second. `shotSpeed` is absent
 * on the weapons with no projectile at all (the lasers, rail guns, Gauss cannons and
 * mine launchers), as is `reloadTime` on the ones that never reload.
 * Engineering base stats: the values a blueprint recipe scales, so a recipe that names
 * one needs it here. `engineHeatRate` and `fsdHeatRate` are waste heat per second (at
 * top speed, and while charging a jump); `refuelRate` is a fuel scoop's tonnes per
 * second; `shieldBankReinforcement` / `shieldBankHeat` / `shieldBankSpinUp` /
 * `shieldBankDuration` are one shield cell's MJ per second, heat per use, and seconds
 * before and of the reinforcement; `scannerRange` is metres (a utility scanner's scan
 * distance, a sensor suite's typical emission range — the panel shows the latter in km),
 * `scanAngle` degrees and `scanTime` seconds; `probeRadius` is the Detailed Surface
 * Scanner's probe radius as a percentage, which is how the game stores that one;
 * `interdictorFacingLimit` is degrees and `interdictorRange` is seconds to intercept.
 * A shield generator's `distributorDraw` is the systems-capacitor cost of 1 MJ/s of
 * regeneration, the stat a journal labels `EnergyPerRegen`.
 * `cost` is the standard purchase price in credits before any station discount;
 * it is omitted, never zeroed, where no registry publishes one (0 is a real price).
 * `unknownStats` names the stat fields this record omits because the value is
 * *unknown* rather than because the module has none — a cargo rack has no
 * `powerDraw`, but a Discovery Scanner has one nobody publishes. Present only where
 * an absence needs that said; a field named here is always absent from the record,
 * so sourcing a value means deleting its name here in the same change. The difference
 * is load-bearing for engineering: a recipe that scales a stat the module does not have
 * is inert, while one that scales a stat named here is refused outright.
 *
 * Full provenance: data/ships/SOURCES.md.
 */
[
